#delete_macros:
#delete_macros { $macros_to_be_deleted } removes previously defined macros by name, irrespective of how many arguments they take -- every arity sharing that name is deleted. For example:

#delete_macros { LP_Factor SW ZE }

deletes any previously defined macros named LP_Factor, SW and ZE.

#m_unique:
#m_unique $string assigns a unique parameter name to $string within a macro body, at the point the macro is expanded -- letting a macro define new internal parameters without those names clashing across multiple expansions of the same macro. For example:

macro Some_macro(v) { prm #m_unique a = Cos(Th); : v }

'a' is assigned a unique parameter name scoped to that macro body's own expansion. The Robust_Refinement and TCHZ_Peak_Type macros (in Topas.inc) are worked examples of its use -- Robust_Refinement, for instance, declares roughly a dozen internal parameters (test, N, p0..p10, t) each via prm #m_unique, so the macro can be used more than once in the same INP file without its own internal working parameters colliding with each other.

#prm:
#prm defines a pre-processor parameter (a "hash parameter"), evaluated purely at the pre-processor stage while the INP file is being loaded -- entirely separate from, and unknown to, the kernel's prm/local parameters. #prm's can be a function of other #prm's, and are typically used inside #if/#elseif/#m_if/#m_elseif conditions to drive pre-processor-level INP file manipulation (see test_examples\hash_prm.inp). #out $prm_name outputs the current value of a #prm directly into the pre-processed text, at the position of the #out directive -- useful both for inspecting what a #prm evaluated to, and for forcing a macro argument to be evaluated to a plain value rather than substituted as raw unevaluated text. For example:

#prm a = Constant(Rand(0,1));
#out a

outputs a random number between 0 and 1 at that point in the file (wrapping in Constant(...) means every reference to a in the same file sees the same value). Pre-processed output (showing exactly what every #prm/#out expanded to) can be inspected in TOPAS.LOG (TA.EXE) or TC.LOG (TC.EXE).

#out is also the key to writing a macro that repeats/modifies text correctly: naively recursive macros without #out produce nested, unevaluated expressions (e.g. 'A (((10)-1)-1)' for a countdown macro), because plain macro arguments are substituted as raw text, not evaluated. Routing the value through a #prm and #out forces it to be evaluated to a plain number first:

macro Repeat(& n) {
  #if (n > 0)
    #prm a = n;
    #prm am1 = n - 1;
    A #out a
    Repeat(#out am1)
  #endif
}
Repeat(10)

which correctly outputs 'A 10  A 9  A 8 ... A 1' instead of the unevaluated nested form. A shorter equivalent uses the Numeric(n) helper macro: 'macro Numeric(& n) { #prm a = n; #out a }'.

Bkg_at:
Bkg_at(x) returns the value of the Chebyshev background function bkg at the x-axis position x. It is used inside penalty functions to restrain bkg's shape at specific points when it misbehaves during Pawley, Le Bail or deconvolution refinements (see bkg) and the Deconvolution_Bkg_Penalty macro isn't sufficient or applicable. For example, applied to TOF data with:

bkg @ 0.443519294` 0.0200324829` 0.0113774736`

the following penalties restrain the value of bkg at x=2036, x=9000 and x=14600 to 0.43, 0.50 and 0.50 respectively:

penalty = 1000000 (Bkg_at(2036) - 0.43)^2;
penalty = 1000000 (Bkg_at(9000) - 0.50)^2;
penalty = 1000000 (Bkg_at(14600) - 0.50)^2; Typically only two to three such Bkg_at penalties are necessary to stabilize a background, and the target values used are usually determined graphically by inspecting the data.

TPre_processor:
TPre_processor (see Chapter 21, 'Macros and Include files') groups the pre-processor directives -- everything expanded, evaluated, or resolved BEFORE the kernel ever sees the INP file's text, as opposed to ordinary keywords/parameters, which the kernel parses and can refine. Three broad families:

the macro directive (macro $name { ... }, plus #include for loading macro definitions from a file and #delete_macros for removing previously defined ones);

the pre-processor's conditional/flag family -- #define/#undef set and clear a flag, #if/#elseif/#else/#endif/#ifdef/#ifndef branch on it (or on a general equation), #prm/#out define and inspect pre-processor-only 'hash parameters' driving those conditions, and #seed makes pre-processor-stage randomization reproducible;

and the directives invoked during macro expansion -- #m_if/#m_elseif/#m_else/#m_endif are the macro-body equivalent of the plain conditional family, #m_ifarg (with its five test forms) tests a macro argument's shape, and #m_argu/#m_unique_not_refine/#m_first_word transform a macro argument's value in place.

All of these share one defining trait: they run once, while TOPAS is still assembling the final text it will parse (visible afterward in TOPAS.LOG/TC.LOG) -- none of them are refined, and none of them exist anymore once the kernel takes over.

Value_at_X:
Value_at_X(object, x) returns object's value at x-axis position x; object can be a parameter or a user_y object. For example, to keep a background close to the high-angle end of a pattern during PDF generation:

user_y u capillary.xy fit_obj = (p0 + p1 X) u;
bkg @ 0 0 0
penalty = 1000 (Bkg_at(X2) + (p0 + p1 X2) Value_at_X(u, X2) - Yobs_at(X2))^2;

activate:
activate !E, a child of pick_atoms, is the condition that must evaluate to non-zero for that pick_atoms block's operations (choose_from/choose_to/choose_randomly/omit/displace/insert) to run -- for example:

activate = Mod(Cycle_Iter, 20) == 0;

runs it only once every 20 iterations. See pick_atoms for the overall mechanism.

add_to_cloud_N:
add_to_cloud_N !E [add_to_cloud_when !E], used in the GUI, adds the current electron-density cloud to a running average cloud kept for display purposes -- add_to_cloud_N is the number of most recent clouds included in that running average, and add_to_cloud_when is the condition controlling when the current cloud is added to it. For example:

add_to_cloud_N 10 add_to_cloud_when = Mod(Cycle_Iter, 2);

keeps a running average of the last 10 clouds, updated every other iteration.

add_to_cloud_when:
add_to_cloud_when !E, paired with add_to_cloud_N !E, is a GUI-related charge-flipping keyword: the current electron-density cloud is added to a running average cloud shown in the GUI for display purposes. add_to_cloud_N sets how many of the most recent clouds are included in that running average; add_to_cloud_when is an equation that determines when the current cloud should be added to it. For example:

add_to_cloud_N 10 add_to_cloud_when = Mod(Cycle_Iter, 2);

which adds a cloud to the average every second iteration. Averaged clouds eliminate noise and are effective as long as the cloud remains stationary, which is generally the case -- but add_to_phases_of_weak_reflections can produce translations of the cloud, so it should not be used at the same time as cloud averaging.

ai_closest_N:
atomic_interaction N E | ai_anti_bump N defines a named site-to-site interaction (name N) between sites identified by ai_sites_1 $sites_1 and ai_sites_2 $sites_2. For atomic_interaction, E is a user-supplied interaction equation that can be a function of R (the distance in Angstrom between the two atoms, updated automatically whenever the dependent fractional coordinates change) and Ri; the name N can then be used elsewhere, including in penalty equations. ai_anti_bump is a faster, purpose-built variant that uses an internal C++ anti-bump interaction equation instead of a user equation -- for anti-bumping alone it is faster than a hand-written atomic_interaction, and is what the AI_Anti_Bump macro uses internally. ai_no_self_interation prevents interactions between equivalent positions of the same site, useful when a general position is being used to describe what is really a special position. ai_closest_N !E sorts the $sites_1/$sites_2 interactions by distance and keeps only the closest ai_closest_N of them; ai_radius !E instead keeps only interactions within that distance; if both are defined, the union of both sets of interactions is considered. ai_exclude_eq_0 restricts interactions to those NOT involving the first (0th) equivalent position in $sites_2 -- e.g. with ai_sites_1 Pb and ai_sites_2 "O1 O2", this keeps Pb:0-O1:n and Pb:0-O2:n only for n != 0; ai_only_eq_0 does the opposite, keeping only interactions between equivalent position 0 on both sides.

The equation can additionally use the functions AI_R(#ri) (distance to the atom with Ri=#ri), AI_R_CM (distance to the geometric center of the current atom and $sites_2), AI_Flatten(#toll) (sum of perpendicular distances to a best-fit plane through the current atom and $sites_2), AI_Cos_Angle(#ri1, #ri2) and AI_Angle(#ri1, #ri2) (cosine, or degrees, of the angle at the current atom between the atoms Ri=#ri1 and Ri=#ri2) -- see examples benzene_ai1.inp, benzene_ai2.inp and benzene_ai3.inp. A typical anti-bumping restraint applied only for the first 10 iterations of a cycle looks like:

atomic_interaction ai1 = If(R < 3, (R-3)^2, 0);
ai_exclude_eq_0 ai_sites_1 C* ai_sites_2 C* ai_radius 3
penalty = If(Cycle_Iter < 10, ai1, 0);

