cf_in_A_matrix:
cf_in_A_matrix $file [scale_Aij !E] loads charge-flipping input from an A-matrix file created by out_A_matrix from a previous Pawley refinement (see Out_for_cf(file), the topas.inc macro that wraps this workflow). The correlations in that file are used to partition intensities during each charge-flipping iteration, applied to both the structure factors CF uses and the tangent formula's Eh values -- specifically, TOPAS uses the diagonally normalized A-matrix (called D) to modify the normalized structure factors Eh during charge flipping, which gives better results for powder data than using reflections in SHELX hkl format (Whitfield & Coelho, 2016). scale_Aij modifies the A-matrix off-diagonal coefficients used in that partitioning; it defaults to 0 (equivalent to using a plain Pawley-generated hkl file, i.e. no partitioning), and can instead be set to use the off-diagonal correlations:

scale_Aij = Get(Aij);
scale_Aij = Get(Aij)^2; CF on powder data can also be started from an ordinary hkl file instead, without cf_in_A_matrix at all. See examples cf\cf-cime.inp (the charge-flipping run) and cf\cf-cime-pawley.inp (the Pawley refinement that produces the *.A file it reads).

chi2:
chi2 allows refinement to minimize a user-defined chi2 instead of the standard least-squares objective function, as a function of the reserved parameter names X, Yobs, Ycalc and SigmaYobs (xdd_sum can also be used as part of building such an expression, being similarly a function of these names) -- for example, a normal least-squares formulation can be written as:

xdd_sum numerator = (Yobs - Ycalc)^2 / Max(Yobs, 1);
xdd_sum denominator = Yobs;
chi2 = 100 Sqrt(numerator / denominator);

Because refining on an arbitrary chi2 needs its first and second derivatives with respect to each independent parameter, Ycalc within chi2 is approximated with a first-order Taylor expansion around the parameter vector, so the full Ycalc need only be calculated once per refinement iteration rather than many times over; chi2 is then treated internally as a penalty, with a definite minimum bracketed per parameter and located via inverse parabolic interpolation, yielding first- and second-order derivatives that are solved using the BFGS/BCCG-with-Marquardt-constant machinery. Example test_examples\chi2-ceo2.inp compares refinement with and without a user-defined chi2, showing the convergence path differs from normal least squares even when the final Rwp is similar.

convolute_X_recal:
convolute_X_recal !E is a child of pdf_convolute (within Tpdf_convolute, alongside min_X and max_X), used in PDF refinement to control a user-defined convolution's x-axis extent. It is not described in prose anywhere in the Technical Reference manual -- only listed in the keyword tree -- so the following is inferred from its real usage in test_examples\pdf\beq-3.inp rather than documented behavior:

prm PDF_CONVOLUTE##a 40 min 1.0e-6 del 1 val_on_continue = Rand(5, 100);
pdf_convolute = Exp(-a X);
  min_X 0
  max_X = -Ln(0.001) / a;
  convolute_X_recal 1

Here max_X is an equation of the refined/randomized parameter a, so the convolution's x-axis extent changes as a changes. convolute_X_recal appears to force TOPAS to recalculate that extent (rather than treating min_X/max_X as fixed at their first-evaluated value), which would otherwise be an easy case to get wrong when max_X isn't a plain constant. Treat this as a reasonable inference from the example, not a manual-confirmed description.

create_pks_fn:
create_pks_fn $fn_name (an xo_Is dependent, used alongside create_pks_name) additionally appends a penalty to every peak created by create_pks_name, written as a call to a user-defined function fn_name -- e.g. the OUT file ends up with lines like:

xo 5.00 I a25_ 0.00217 penalty = dfn(5,a25_,a26_);

for each created peak.

delete_observed_reflections:
delete_observed_reflections = E; removes observed reflections before they enter charge flipping, as a boolean function of D_spacing (and any other per-reflection quantity), for example:

delete_observed_reflections = D_spacing < 1.1;

In a Pawley refinement the calculated intensities at the low d-spacing edge are often in error to a large extent, so it is best to remove those unreliable high-angle reflections before running CF on the resulting .A/hkl file, e.g.:

delete_observed_reflections = D_spacing < 1.134;

It can also be ramped over the CF run rather than fixed, e.g. within a Ramp(...)-driven macro cycling Nr iterations:

delete_observed_reflections = D_spacing < #;

with # supplied per-cycle alongside add_to_phases_of_weak_reflections, flip_regime_2 and symmetry_obey_0_to_1. See also min_d and extend_calculated_sphere_to, which control the resolution range from the other end.

dr:
dr !E, a child of pdf_generate, sets the r-axis step size (delta-r) of the generated G(r), for example:

dr = 0.01;

See pdf_generate for the overall PDF-generation process.

existing_prm:
existing_prm E; modifies an already-declared prm/local parameter in place, evaluated sequentially (each existing_prm statement sees the result of the ones before it) -- see e.g. the K_Factor_WP macro in topas.inc, which uses:

existing_prm k_factor_sum_wps_ += k_factor_wp_;

to accumulate a running total across phases. For example:

local a 1
existing_prm a += 1;
existing_prm a /= 2;
existing_prm a = 3 (a + 1);
prm = a; : 0

gives prm = a; : 6.00000 -- i.e. a starts at 1, becomes 2 (+= 1), then 1 (/= 2), then 3 * (1 + 1) = 6 (= 3 (a + 1)). Allowed operators for existing_prm are +=, -=, *=, /= and ^= (the manual's operator table lists this last group as "+=, -=, *-, /= and ^=", which is almost certainly a typo for *= given every other operator in the group ends in =). A common use is a running counter across a for-loop or Create_XDDs()-generated block, e.g.:

existing_prm i += 1;

inside a 'for xdds { ... }' loop.

flip_equation:
flip_equation = E; defines a user-written charge-flipping perturbation regime -- the rule that modifies the electron density each CF iteration wherever it sits below threshold. Only one flipping regime can be active at a time: the two built-in ones (flip_regime_2, flip_regime_3) or this user-defined one. If none of the three is set, TOPAS falls back to the standard Oszlanyi & Suto flip: density is negated wherever it is below Get(threshold), and left unchanged otherwise. flip_equation lets you write that rule explicitly, for example:

flip_equation = If(Get(density) < Get(threshold), -Get(density), Get(density));

which reproduces the default behavior directly. neutron_data uses a variant of this same form as its built-in default when working with neutron scattering lengths:

flip_equation = If(And(Get(density) < Get(threshold), Get(density) > 0.4 Get(min_density)), -Get(density), Get(density));

(the 0.4 factor there is adjustable via flip_neutron). Custom flipping regimes are mainly useful for damping high electron densities -- e.g. to avoid the tangent formula collapsing onto a single-heavy-atom ("uranium atom") solution on difficult structures or poor-resolution data -- as an alternative to using pick_atoms for the same purpose.

fourier_map:
fourier_map !E, when non-zero, triggers a Fourier map to be calculated on refinement termination and displayed in the OpenGL window; maps can be calculated for X-ray or neutron data, single crystal or powder (see examples fourier-map-ae14.inp and fourier-map-cime.inp). The type of map is controlled by fourier_map_formula, which can be a function of the reserved parameter names Fcalc, Fobs and D_spacing -- it defaults to:

fourier_map_formula = Fobs;

(a conventional Fo map), but can be set to something like:

fourier_map_formula = 2 Fobs - Fcalc;

for a 2Fo-Fc-style difference map. For single-crystal data, Fobs is the observed structure modulus directly; for powder data, Fobs is instead calculated via the Rietveld decomposition formula. Structure factor phases used in the map always come from Fcalc, not Fobs. Reflections missing from within the observed Ewald sphere have Fobs set equal to Fcalc (i.e. contribute no difference signal); extend_calculated_sphere_to can be used to extend the Ewald sphere so fewer reflections fall into this missing-data case. min_grid_spacing sets the finest grid spacing used when calculating the map (useful for obtaining a smoother map for graphical purposes; it defaults to the smaller of min_d/2 and any explicitly given min_grid_spacing). Any scale_pks definitions are removed from Fobs before mapping; if scale_pks evaluates to zero for a particular reflection, Fobs is set to Fcalc for that reflection too, and the number of such reflections is reported on. See also correct_for_atomic_scattering_factors and f_atom_type/f_atom_quantity, which control the atomic-scattering-factor corrections applied when computing the structure factors that feed into the map.

fraction_density_to_flip:
fraction_density_to_flip !E; the amount of electron density charge-flipped each iteration is fractionally based rather than being tied to a fixed density value. A value of 0.6, for example, sets the flip threshold delta such that the sign of the lowest 60% of charge (by density) is changed. Get(threshold) can be used to retrieve the current value of delta in equations (e.g. within a custom flip_equation). fraction_density_to_flip is commonly ramped down over a CF run, e.g.:

fraction_density_to_flip = Ramp(0.85, 0.8, 100);

gradually reducing perturbation as the solution converges -- similar in spirit to a simulated-annealing temperature schedule alongside fraction_reflections_weak, flip_regime_2/flip_regime_3, and symmetry_obey_0_to_1.

ft_conv_im:
ft_conv_im E, a sibling of ft_conv_re under ft_conv_re_im (see also ft_min, ft_x_axis_range), defines the imaginary part of the Fourier Transform (FT) of a response function that is convoluted into phase peaks via FFT. Where the plain ft_conv shorthand (equal to 'ft_conv_re_im ft_conv_re') only sets a real-valued transform -- adequate for symmetric response functions like a Gaussian or Lorentzian -- ft_conv_im is needed when the response function being convoluted is asymmetric, since an asymmetric function's Fourier transform has a non-zero imaginary component. For example, to convolute a (symmetric) Voigt into a phase, only the real part is needed:

ft_conv = Exp(-(Pi FT_K gfwhm)^2 / (4 Ln(2)) - Pi FT_K lfwhm);
ft_min = 1e-8; ' this is the default; ft_min is optional
ft_x_axis_range = 40 lfwhm;

(here the convolution theorem is used, multiplying the FT of a Gaussian by the FT of a Lorentzian). More than one FT-based convolution can be defined; when several are, TOPAS internally applies the convolution theorem to combine them. See examples ALVO4A.INP and VOIGT.INP, and see also WPPM_ft_conv_im, the analogous imaginary-part keyword used by the WPPM microstructure-analysis convolutions.

ft_conv_re:
ft_conv_re E, a sibling of ft_conv_im under ft_conv_re_im (see also ft_min, ft_x_axis_range), defines the real part of the Fourier Transform (FT) of a response function that is convoluted into phase peaks via FFT -- the FFT approach is used instead of direct convolution when the response function's transform is more convenient/faster to express or combine than the function itself. The plain ft_conv keyword is shorthand for 'ft_conv_re_im ft_conv_re' (i.e. it sets the real part directly, appropriate for symmetric response functions where the imaginary part is zero). For example, to convolute a Voigt into a phase by multiplying the FT of a Gaussian by the FT of a Lorentzian (the convolution theorem):

ft_conv = Exp(-(Pi FT_K gfwhm)^2 / (4 Ln(2)) - Pi FT_K lfwhm);
ft_min = 1e-8; ' this is the default; ft_min is optional
ft_x_axis_range = 40 lfwhm;

More than one FT-based convolution can be defined at once; TOPAS automatically combines them using the convolution theorem. Use ft_conv_re (optionally with ft_conv_im) directly instead of the ft_conv shorthand when the transform needs finer control or an explicit imaginary component. See examples ALVO4A.INP and VOIGT.INP, and see also WPPM_ft_conv_re, the analogous keyword used by the WPPM microstructure-analysis convolutions.

ft_min:
ft_min !E is an optional sibling of ft_conv_re/ft_conv_im/ft_x_axis_range, used with FFT-based phase-peak convolutions (ft_conv). It defaults to 1e-8 and does not need to be set explicitly unless a different cutoff is wanted:

ft_conv = Exp(-(Pi FT_K gfwhm)^2 / (4 Ln(2)) - Pi FT_K lfwhm);
ft_min = 1e-8; ' this is the default; ft_min is optional
ft_x_axis_range = 40 lfwhm;

The manual does not describe ft_min's exact mechanics in prose beyond noting its default value; by analogy with the similarly-named WPPM_break_on_small (which terminates the WPPM Fourier transform calculation once the transform's value falls below a small fraction of its value at zero), ft_min most likely serves as a small-value cutoff/tolerance controlling when the FT calculation for ft_conv is considered negligible and can stop -- treat this as inference rather than confirmed documented behavior. See examples ALVO4A.INP and VOIGT.INP.

ft_x_axis_range:
ft_x_axis_range !E is a sibling of ft_conv_re/ft_conv_im/ft_min, used with FFT-based phase-peak convolutions (ft_conv). It sets the x-axis extent over which the FFT-convoluted response function is calculated -- analogous to the min_X/max_X pair used by other convolution mechanisms (e.g. user_defined_convolution), but expressed as a single total range rather than separate min/max bounds. For example, when convoluting a Voigt into a phase via the convolution theorem:

ft_conv = Exp(-(Pi FT_K gfwhm)^2 / (4 Ln(2)) - Pi FT_K lfwhm);
ft_min = 1e-8; ' this is the default; ft_min is optional
ft_x_axis_range = 40 lfwhm;

here the range is written as a multiple of the Lorentzian FWHM (lfwhm), giving enough x-axis extent to capture the Voigt's tails without wasting computation far beyond them. This is similar in spirit to WPPM_th2_range, which serves the same range-limiting purpose for WPPM_ft_conv. See examples ALVO4A.INP and VOIGT.INP.

gr_sst_file:
gr_sst_file = "File", a child of pdf_generate, sets the base filename that the generated G(r) is written to; an .sst extension is appended automatically, for example:

gr_sst_file = "gr";

writes to gr.sst. See pdf_generate for the overall PDF-generation process.

hkl_Re_Im:
hkl_Re_Im #h #k #l #Re #Im is a repeatable child of phase_penalties $sites N, used in structure solution to restrain specific reflections' phases toward known/assumed values. Each hkl_Re_Im line supplies a user-defined hkl (#h #k #l) plus the real and imaginary parts (#Re #Im) of its assigned structure factor s. These hkls should be chosen with high intensity, large d-spacing, and isolated from other peaks (to avoid peak overlap) -- origin-defining hkls are a typical choice. For example:

penalty = pp1;
phase_penalties * pp1
load hkl_Re_Im {
  0   1   2   1  0
  1   0  -2   1  0
  1  -2  -1   1  0
}

phase_penalties computes, per hkl, a penalty term Pp,hkl comparing the assigned phase s (from Re/Im here) against the calculated phase c (from the sites in $sites): zero if the two phases are within +/-45 degrees of each other, and proportional to d * Ic,hkl^2 * (phi_s - phi_c)^2 otherwise. The named sum N (pp1 in the example) can then be used in penalty equations to restrain the structure solution toward those known phases. See examples AE14-12.INP and AE5-AUTO.INP.

index_x0:
index_x0 !E, used during indexing (TIndexing), defines Xhh in the reciprocal lattice equation used to seed the search -- effectively an initial guess for 1/d^2 of the very first indexable line. In a triclinic lattice, the largest observed d-spacing can often safely be indexed as 100, 200, etc., so setting:

index_x0 = 1/(dmax)^2;

(or, if the data is in 2*Theta degrees):

index_x0 = (2 Sin(2*Thetamin * Pi/360) / wavelength)^2;

speeds up the indexing process and increases the chance of finding the correct solution, provided that first line can be indexed as 100 (see example ex13.inp). The macros Index_x0_from_d and Index_x0_from_th2 simplify setting up index_x0 from a d-spacing or a 2*Theta value respectively.

insert:
insert !E, a child of pick_atoms, inserts the operated-on atoms back into the electron density; a value of 1 inserts them with an intensity equal to the average of the picked atoms, while values less than 1 decrease the inserted intensity. When insert is defined, omit is internally defined too if not already set, so atoms are removed before being re-inserted by default. displace, if also defined, is applied before insert, letting picked atoms be randomly repositioned as well as re-scaled, e.g.:

pick_atoms * choose_to 5 insert = Rand(-.1, 1);

to limit uranium-atom solutions. See pick_atoms for the overall mechanism.

load:
load, move_to $ and for { .. } are three related keywords for working with the internal data tree (see Chapter 2). load { } allows keywords of the same type to be entered once instead of repeated -- for example:

xdd exclude 20 22  exclude 32 35  exclude 45 47

can be rewritten as:

xdd load exclude { 20 22 32 35 45 47 }

In some cases the attribute equations (min, max, del, update, stop_when, val_on_continue) are loaded by the parameter itself rather than by load, e.g.:

prm t 0.01 val_on_continue = Rand(-Pi, Pi); min 0.4 max 0.5

has the prm itself load its own attributes -- whereas when load is used and the load block does NOT define min/max for a given parameter, that parameter loads its own already-set attributes instead, e.g. in:

load sh_Cij_prm { y00 !sh_c00 1  y20 sh_c20 0.26202642 min 0 max 1  y40 sh_c40 0.06823548 ... }

y00 and y40 load their own defaults since no min/max is given for them here, while y20 gets the min/max explicitly given in this load block.

move_to $keyword lets you enter parameter attributes without first loading the parameter itself ($keyword can be any object in the internal data tree) -- for example, the site-dependent ADPs_Keep_PD macro uses:

move_to u12  min = -Sqrt(Get(u11) Get(u22));  max = Sqrt(Get(u11) Get(u22));

to set min/max limits on u12 without reloading it (see also the Keep_Atom_Within_Box macro).

for $node { ... } iterates over all nodes of the internal data tree, e.g.:

for site_recs { beq @ 1 }

and is most commonly seen as 'for strs { ... }' or 'for xdds { ... }', applying the same INP text to every str or xdd respectively, which is central to writing compact macros that operate uniformly across many phases or patterns. Using a parameter name prefixed with $ inside a for loop creates a distinct local parameter per iteration rather than one shared parameter across all of them:

for strs { CS_L(@, 100) }

expands to a single shared crystallite-size parameter used by every str in the loop, whereas:

for strs { CS_L($cs, 100) }

expands to a unique cs parameter per str. The drawback of using $ this way is that only the last such local value is written back to the OUT file by default -- the load_save_locals keyword remedies this by saving and restoring every per-iteration local value separately.

modify_initial_phases:
modify_initial_phases !E works alongside set_initial_phases_to $file, which sets initial phases to those appearing in $file -- typically a *.FC file saved from a previous charge-flipping run. modify_initial_phases is executed every CF iteration and can be used to restrain the phases toward those in $file rather than leaving them completely free. For example:

modify_initial_phases = Get(initial_phase) + Min(Abs(Get(phase_difference)), 45);

where Get(phase_difference) is the difference between the current phase and the initial phase, valued between +/-90 degrees -- so this example caps how far the phase is allowed to drift from its initial value each iteration (at most 45 degrees) rather than snapping it back exactly. modify_initial_phases can be used, for example, to constrain phases to values already determined by high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM; Baerlocher et al., 2007).

normals_plot:
An OpenGL plot of lattice plane normals, with each normal's length set by normals_plot -- a per-hkl equation (evaluated using the reserved H, K, L Miller-index variables, the same way str_hkl_angle/phase_penalties equations are) whose value is plotted against each reflection's plane normal. Used together with spherical_harmonics_hkl to visualize an anisotropic peak-shape/preferred-orientation correction as a function of hkl direction, e.g. `normals_plot = sh;` to plot the spherical-harmonics correction, or an arbitrary expression like:

normals_plot = Abs(H*K + L^2) + 1;

-- see test_examples\clay.inp. normals_plot_min_d sets a minimum d-spacing cutoff below which reflections are left out of the plot, keeping the (usually very dense) high-angle/low-d region from cluttering it.

normals_plot_min_d:
An OpenGL plot of lattice plane normals, with each normal's length set by normals_plot -- a per-hkl equation (evaluated using the reserved H, K, L Miller-index variables, the same way str_hkl_angle/phase_penalties equations are) whose value is plotted against each reflection's plane normal. Used together with spherical_harmonics_hkl to visualize an anisotropic peak-shape/preferred-orientation correction as a function of hkl direction, e.g. `normals_plot = sh;` to plot the spherical-harmonics correction, or an arbitrary expression like:

normals_plot = Abs(H*K + L^2) + 1;

-- see test_examples\clay.inp. normals_plot_min_d sets a minimum d-spacing cutoff below which reflections are left out of the plot, keeping the (usually very dense) high-angle/low-d region from cluttering it.

omit:
omit !E, a child of pick_atoms, removes operated-on atoms from the electron density; a value less than 1 partially removes an atom, while a value greater than 1 changes the sign of its electron density. Randomly omitting a few of the highest-intensity picked atoms:

pick_atoms * choose_to 5 omit = Rand(1, 1.1);

is an effective way to prevent spurious high-density ('uranium atom') solutions -- this technique is known as 'random omit maps' in ShelXD (Schneider and Sheldrick, 2002). See example cf\cf-1a7y-omit.inp, and pick_atoms for the overall mechanism.

omit_hkls:
omit_hkls = E; allows for the filtering of hkls using the reserved parameter names H, K, L and D_spacing -- any reflection for which the equation evaluates non-zero is omitted. More than one omit_hkls can be defined (each is applied independently), for example:

omit_hkls = If(And(H==0, K==0), 1, 0);

which omits every 00l reflection.

out_dependences:
out_dependences $user_string outputs, on refinement termination, the list of independently refined parameters that the most previously defined prm or local ultimately depends on -- for example, given:

prm d 1; prm e 1; prm f 1; prm c = e + f; prm b = d + e; prm a = b + c; out_dependences a_tag;

standard output lists a_tag against the internal names of d, e and f (the independent parameters a transitively depends on through b and c), each preceded by the object name (e.g. the parameter's tag, such as prm_10).

out_file:
out_file = $E determines the name of the OUT file written on refinement termination (a copy of the INP file with updated parameter values); it defaults to the INP file's name but with an OUT extension. It must evaluate to a string, so it has to be assigned via '=' with a proper string equation:

out_file aac.out
out_file = aac.out;

both throw an exception, whereas:

out_file = "aac.out";
out_file = String(aac.out);
out_file = If(Get(r_wp) < 10, "aac.out", "");

are all valid.

It is primarily useful together with num_runs (which restarts refinement multiple times within one execution): if num_runs > 1 and out_file is left undefined, no OUT file is saved at all, and the INP file is not updated between runs either -- useful when intermediate OUT output isn't wanted, and information can instead be exchanged between runs via the out keyword and #include. The standard Save_Best macro uses out_file conditionally, only saving an OUT file when the current run's Rwp improves on the best Rwp seen so far across runs (tracked via a value written out with the out keyword and read back with #include on the next run).

pdf_for_pairs:
pdf_for_pairs $sites_1 $sites_2 lets a PDF refinement assign a specific peak-width (via pdf_gauss_fwhm) or other treatment to a particular pair of site groups, overriding the default beq-based broadening for just those atom-atom interactions. $sites_1/$sites_2 are wildcard site strings (e.g. '"V* Al* !O2"' -- '!' excludes a match, see general wildcard-string rules). Multiple pdf_for_pairs blocks can be defined; pdf_only_eq_0 restricts a block to only equivalent position 0 (i.e. intra- rather than inter-molecule pairs). If every pair of interest is covered by some pdf_for_pairs, the sites' beq's go unused (TOPAS reports which beq's are unused).

For example, to give Al1-O bonds within the same molecule (equivalent position 0) a narrow FWHM different from beq (see pdf-2.inp):

prm intra_molec 0.01 min 1e-6
pdf_for_pairs Al1 "O1 O2 O3 O4 O5 O6" pdf_only_eq_0 pdf_gauss_fwhm = intra_molec;

and to additionally give the inter-molecule Al1-O interactions their own separate width:

prm inter_molec 0.1 min 1e-6
prm intra_molec 0.01 min 1e-6
pdf_for_pairs Al1 "O1 O2 O3 O4 O5 O6" pdf_only_eq_0 pdf_gauss_fwhm = intra_molec;
pdf_for_pairs Al1 "O1 O2 O3 O4 O5 O6" pdf_gauss_fwhm = inter_molec;

Rules for combining pdf_for_pairs with plain beq (per site-pair): if only beq is used, beq covers all three cases (intra-eq0, inter, non-eq0); if only a plain pdf_for_pairs (no pdf_only_eq_0) is used, it covers all three; if only pdf_only_eq_0 is used, beq still covers the non-eq0 cases and pdf_for_pairs covers eq0; if both a plain and an eq0-restricted pdf_for_pairs are defined for the same pair, beq is ignored entirely and the two pdf_for_pairs blocks split the coverage. An exception is thrown if the same interaction ends up referenced by more than one pdf_for_pairs (e.g. defining both 'pdf_for_pairs Al1 "O1...O6" pdf_only_eq_0' and 'pdf_for_pairs Al1 O1 pdf_only_eq_0' -- Al1-O1 eq0 would be ambiguous). pdf_info displays the resulting per-pair assignment as a matrix, which is useful for confirming what's actually in effect. pdf_convolute can also be used as a dependent of pdf_for_pairs, though this is slower than defining it at the global/xdd/str level. See examples pdf-1.inp, pdf-2.inp, beq-3.inp (created by beq-3-create.inp).

pen_weight:
pen_weight lets the automatically-computed KP/KR weighting of Eq. (4-6)/(4-7) be overridden directly, and is set by the macro Pen_Wt, which in turn calls the user-definable macro Write_Pen_Wt(Aii, Ai, Pii, Pi). The default behaviour of Write_Pen_Wt is equivalent to:

pen_weight = If(Pii < 1e-14 Aii, 0, 1.05 Aii/(Pii + Aii Min(Pi/Ai, 0.05)));

where Aii and Ai correspond to the observed-data terms A0,ii and Y0,i, while Pii and Pi correspond to either AP,ii/YP,i (for the penalty weight KP) or AR,ii/YR,i (for the restraint weight KR), depending on which weight is being computed.

Overriding pen_weight is useful when the default formula's heuristic for balancing data against penalties/restraints does not suit a particular problem -- for example, ShelX-style restraints are reproduced with the simpler fixed choice:

pen_weight = 1;

combined with penalties_weighting_K1 set from Rwp/Rexp. See also the Deconvolution_Init macro, which overrides pen_weight to a fixed value of 1 as part of setting up deconvolution refinement.

penalties_weighting_K1:
penalties_weighting_K1 defines the user-controlled weighting K1 applied to all penalty functions in the objective function of Eq. (4-3) (default value 1; typical values range from 0.1 to 2). Along with KP (which gives approximately equal weight to the sum of inverse-error terms in the parameters from the data and from the penalties, computed via Eq. (4-6) to avoid numerical instability) and KR (the analogous weight for restraints, via Eq. (4-7)), it determines how strongly penalties compete against the observed-data term (and against restraints) during minimization.

When observed data Yo is absent, KR and KP are both set to 1; penalties_weighting_K1 remains the user's direct lever over penalty strength in every case. It is one of the keywords, along with penalty, that most directly changes the form of chi2 for the purposes of structure solution, and is used, for example, in ShelX-style restrained refinement as:

penalties_weighting_K1 = (Get(r_wp) / Get(r_exp))^2;

penalty:
penalty and restraint both add a term to the quantity being minimized, and either can express the same condition -- but they are computed differently, which is the reason both exist.

penalty !E defines a penalty function: an equation of any parameter that is added to the minimization sum, for example a bond-length restraint or a boundary condition on a lattice or crystallite-size parameter:

penalty = (lp_ceo2 - 5.41011)^2;

restraint !E defines a restraint Rr, used in the objective function of Eq. (4-3) alongside penalties.

Any restraint can be rewritten as an equivalent penalty by squaring it:

restraint = a (x - b);

is equivalent to:

penalty = a^2 (x - b)^2;

The two are not evaluated identically, however. A penalty is expanded to a second-order Taylor series and its off-diagonal A-matrix terms AP,ij are always set to zero; a restraint is expanded only to first order, and its off-diagonal terms AR,ij are calculated directly (unless approximate_A/BFGS is in use). So restraints typically converge in fewer iterations than the equivalent penalty -- compare rosenbrock-10.inp with rosenbrock-10-restraint.inp -- though that extra off-diagonal calculation can make each iteration slower in wall-clock time.

Penalties are necessary, rather than restraints, for functions that cannot be squared -- negative functions such as the GRS-series atomic interaction.

ShelX-style restraints can be reproduced with:

restraint = Sqrt(w) (yt - y);

where Sqrt(w) is the square root of the ShelX restraint weight, combined with:

pen_weight = 1;
penalties_weighting_K1 = (Get(r_wp) / Get(r_exp))^2;

Weighting: penalties_weighting_K1 sets the weighting K1 applied to all penalties (default 1); restraints are weighted by KR (see penalties_weighting_K1 and pen_weight).

Errors: do_errors excludes both penalties and restraints from the error-calculation A matrix. do_errors_include_penalties includes penalties in it; do_errors_include_restraints includes restraints.

Minimization method: when there is no observed data, or only_penalties is defined, the BFGS method is used by default (overridable with line_min), as BFGS typically converges fastest for 'penalties only' minimization.

phase_penalties:
phase_penalties $sites N restrains specific reflections' calculated phases toward known/assumed values during structure solution. $sites identifies which sites' structure factors are used to compute the calculated phase phi_c (see site-identifying-string rules, section 19.27); the hkl_Re_Im children supply the assigned phase phi_s (as Re/Im) for each hkl of interest. For a single hkl, the penalty is:

Pp,hkl = 0, if phi_s - 45deg < phi_c < phi_s + 45deg
Pp,hkl = d * Ic^2 * (phi_s - phi_c)^2, otherwise

where phi_s is the assigned phase, phi_c the calculated phase, Ic the calculated intensity, and d the reflection's d-spacing. The named sum N (returning the total over all listed hkls) can be used in equations, particularly penalty equations, to actively pull the structure toward reproducing those phases. For example (see AE14-12.INP and AE5-AUTO.INP):

penalty = pp1;
phase_penalties * pp1
load hkl_Re_Im {
  0   1   2   1  0
  1   0  -2   1  0
  1  -2  -1   1  0
}

hkls chosen for phase penalties should be high intensity, large d-spacing, and isolated from other peaks to avoid overlap; origin-defining hkls are a typical choice. accumulate_phases_and_save_to_file $file and accumulate_phases_when !E are related keywords: the former saves the average of the accumulated phases to $file, collecting a new phase whenever accumulate_phases_when evaluates true (it defaults to true, i.e. every iteration). For example, to only accumulate phases (from the best-Rwp state since the last accumulation) once a specific temperature stage is reached:

load temperature { 1 1 1 1 10 }
  move_to_the_next_temperature_regardless_of_the_change_in_rwp
accumulate_phases_and_save_to_file SOME_FILE.TXT
accumulate_phases_when = T == 10;

pick_atoms_when:
pick_atoms_when !E, used in the GUI, triggers atom picking in the OpenGL display when it evaluates to a non-zero value -- for example:

pick_atoms_when = Mod(Cycle_Iter + 1, 10) == 0;

picks atoms every 10 iterations. Picking can also be initiated manually from the Cloud dialog of the OpenGL display, and a text description of the currently picked atoms can be obtained by opening the 'Temporary output' text window of the OpenGL window.

quick_refine_remove:
quick_refine_remove !E gives explicit, per-parameter control over which parameters are removed or reinstated from refinement during a cycle when quick_refine is defined, rather than relying on the automatic scheme of Eq. (4-9) (which does not reinstate parameters until the start of the next cycle). A parameter is removed from refinement when its quick_refine_remove equation evaluates to a non-zero value, and reinstated when it evaluates to zero; it can be a function of the reserved parameters QR_Removed and QR_Num_Times_Consecutively_Small, plus global reserved names like Cycle_Iter, Cycle and T. A typical block-refining pattern reinstates a parameter if it was already removed, otherwise removes it once QR_Num_Times_Consecutively_Small exceeds 2, e.g.:

quick_refine 0.1 quick_refine_remove = If(QR_Removed, 0, If(QR_Num_Times_Consecutively_Small > 2, 1, 0));

This produces close to the lowest achievable chi2 in a short time for most refinements (see pawley1.inp). If quick_refine_remove is left undefined, the default removal scheme of Eq. (4-9) is used instead.

r_max:
r_max !E, a child of pdf_generate, sets the maximum r value that G(r) is generated out to, for example:

r_max = 100;
r_max = 300;

See pdf_generate for the overall PDF-generation process.

randomize_phases_on_new_cycle_by:
randomize_phases_on_new_cycle_by !E (default 0) randomizes the charge-flipping phases by up to the given amount, in degrees, every time a new cycle begins, for example:

randomize_phases_on_new_cycle_by = Rand(-180, 180);

applies a full random phase perturbation each new cycle. It is triggered both by ordinary cycle progression and by break_cycle_if_true, which interrupts charge flipping, sets Cycle_Iter to zero, increments Cycle, and executes randomize_phases_on_new_cycle_by, e.g.:

break_cycle_if_true = Get(iters_since_last_best) > 100; randomize_phases_on_new_cycle_by = Rand(-180, 180);

restarts with fresh random phases whenever 100 iterations pass without a new best solution. See also randomize_initial_phases_by, the equivalent randomization applied only once, at the very start of a run.

restraint:
penalty and restraint both add a term to the quantity being minimized, and either can express the same condition -- but they are computed differently, which is the reason both exist.

penalty !E defines a penalty function: an equation of any parameter that is added to the minimization sum, for example a bond-length restraint or a boundary condition on a lattice or crystallite-size parameter:

penalty = (lp_ceo2 - 5.41011)^2;

restraint !E defines a restraint Rr, used in the objective function of Eq. (4-3) alongside penalties.

Any restraint can be rewritten as an equivalent penalty by squaring it:

restraint = a (x - b);

is equivalent to:

penalty = a^2 (x - b)^2;

The two are not evaluated identically, however. A penalty is expanded to a second-order Taylor series and its off-diagonal A-matrix terms AP,ij are always set to zero; a restraint is expanded only to first order, and its off-diagonal terms AR,ij are calculated directly (unless approximate_A/BFGS is in use). So restraints typically converge in fewer iterations than the equivalent penalty -- compare rosenbrock-10.inp with rosenbrock-10-restraint.inp -- though that extra off-diagonal calculation can make each iteration slower in wall-clock time.

Penalties are necessary, rather than restraints, for functions that cannot be squared -- negative functions such as the GRS-series atomic interaction.

ShelX-style restraints can be reproduced with:

restraint = Sqrt(w) (yt - y);

where Sqrt(w) is the square root of the ShelX restraint weight, combined with:

pen_weight = 1;
penalties_weighting_K1 = (Get(r_wp) / Get(r_exp))^2;

Weighting: penalties_weighting_K1 sets the weighting K1 applied to all penalties (default 1); restraints are weighted by KR (see penalties_weighting_K1 and pen_weight).

Errors: do_errors excludes both penalties and restraints from the error-calculation A matrix. do_errors_include_penalties includes penalties in it; do_errors_include_restraints includes restraints.

Minimization method: when there is no observed data, or only_penalties is defined, the BFGS method is used by default (overridable with line_min), as BFGS typically converges fastest for 'penalties only' minimization.

s:
s E is a site-dependent parameter used with grs_interaction/repulsion_refine (TEnergy_minimization, see also the sibling site parameters g and q). It scales the equation part of a shared grs_interaction, which simplifies setting up repulsion terms that would otherwise need one grs_interaction block per site-pair. Consider three separate interactions:

grs_interaction qi = 3; qj = -2; Al* O* p1 = B1 / R^7; penalty = p1;
grs_interaction qi = 5; qj = -2; V*  O* p2 = B2 / R^7; penalty = p2;
grs_interaction qi = 3; qj =  5; Al* V* p3 = B3 / R^7; penalty = p3;

In repulsion_refine mode, fractional coordinates aren't refined, but their derivatives with respect to the grs_interaction equations are still expensive to compute. The site-level s parameter avoids recalculating this by letting the three interactions collapse into one shared equation:

site Al ... q 3 s s1 1
site V ...  q 5 s s2 1
site O ...  q -2 s s3 1
repulsion_refine
grs_interaction * * c = 1/R^9;
penalty = c;

TOPAS examines the grs_interaction equation (1/R^9 here) and, if it's independent of refined parameters, caches its value for reuse when calculating derivatives with respect to the repulsion parameters -- a significant speedup. s1, s2, s3 here are related to the original B1/B2/B3 coefficients (see s0/sal/sv-style derivations in GRS-ALVO4\SOLVE-1.INP, which computes s values from bond-length Ro values via Sqrt/pow expressions rather than fitting B directly). Examples: ALVO4-GRS-AUTO.INP, GRS-ALVO4\SOLVE-1.INP, GRS-ALVO4\REP-1.INP, GRS-ALVO4\REP-2.INP.

scale_pks:
Scales phase peaks; the following defines a Lorentz-Polarisation correction:

scale_pks = (1 + Cos(c Deg)^2 Cos(2 Th)^2) / (Sin(Th)^2 Cos(Th));

See LP_Factor, Preferred_Orientation and Absorption_With_Sample_Thickness_mm_Intensity macros.

scale_weak_reflections:
scale_weak_reflections !E, used in charge flipping. By default, weak reflection structure factors are set to zero; however, when either scale_weak_reflections or add_to_phases_of_weak_reflections is defined, weak reflection structure factors are instead modified accordingly rather than zeroed, for example:

scale_weak_reflections = Rand(-0.2, 0.4);

scale_weak_reflections or add_to_phases_of_weak_reflections can be a function of D_spacing.

seed:
seed [#] initializes the random number generator, by default with a seed based on the computer clock; if a number # follows seed, the generator is instead seeded with that number, giving reproducible random sequences (e.g. for repeatable simulated-annealing runs). The command-line tc.exe increments the number stored in the file seed-tc.txt each run and saves the incremented value back, so successive command-line runs get different seeds automatically; the GUI version, ta.exe, does the same using seed-tb.txt instead. #seed is the preprocessor-stage equivalent of seed -- it seeds the random number generator while the INP file is still being loaded/expanded, before refinement begins, rather than at the point seed is encountered during normal keyword processing; like seed, an explicit number after #seed overrides the default clock-based seed. #seed_eqn goes further, seeding the generator at the preprocessor stage from the value of an equation rather than a literal number -- for example:

#seed_eqn seed_1 = Rand(0,32767);
prm value_fed_to_random_number_generator = #out seed_1;

to recover the seed used, or:

#seed_eqn seed_2 = Run_Number;

to derive a distinct, reproducible seed per num_runs iteration. The underlying Rand(r1, r2) function extends C++'s native rand() (which only returns integers in [0, 32767]) to a continuous double-precision value uniformly distributed over [r1, r2].

sum_smvs_minus_this:
sum_smvs_minus_this (accessed via 'prm = Get(sum_smvs_minus_this);', a str-dependent value) is Get(sum_smvs) minus the current phase's own smv contribution -- i.e. the sum of every OTHER phase's Sp Mp Vp / Bp. Used to solve one phase's scale factor algebraically from a known elemental or overall weight percent, without that phase's own scale/mass/volume already being folded into the sum it's being solved from -- e.g. rearranging the weight-fraction formula for a phase with a known elemental content known_Zr:

scale = (0.01 known_Zr Get(sum_smvs_minus_this) - s2 v2 z2 - s3 v3 z3) / (v1 (z1 - 0.01 known_Zr m1));

and the Known_Weight_Percent macro, which constrains a phase's scale to a known overall weight percent w:

macro Known_Weight_Percent(& w) { scale = (w / (100 - w)) Get(sum_smvs_minus_this) / (Get(cell_mass) Get(cell_volume)); }

See quant-2.inp and quant-3.inp, and 11-quantitative-analysis.md.

th2_offset:
th2_offset E is used for applying 2Th corrections to phase peaks -- it shifts each peak's position without changing its shape or intensity, and can be a function of H, K, L, M, Xo, Th and D_spacing (Table 2-3), making anisotropic (hkl-dependent) peak shifts possible. The following applies a sample-displacement correction:

th2_offset = -2 Rad(c) Cos(Th) / Rs;

th2_offset is used this way inside the Zero_Error and Specimen_Displacement macros (in topas.inc). An anisotropic peak shift as a function of L looks like:

prm at 0.07 min 0.0001 max 1
prm bt 0.07 min 0.0001 max 1
th2_offset = If(L == 0, at Tan(Th), bt Tan(Th));

When working with multiplicities rather than individual hkls, the Multiplicities_Sum macro can be used instead, e.g.:

prm a 0 th2_offset = Multiplicities_Sum(If(Mod(L, 2) == 0, a Tan(Th), 0));

In large multi-phase/multi-xdd refinements, th2_offset is one of the objects TOPAS can reuse across common str patterns to save memory and time (see approximate_A_check_must_be_zero); if the same str pattern needs different offsets in different xdds, transform_X is an alternative that avoids duplicating th2_offset and the peak summation twice.

track:
track !E is a site-dependent keyword (a sibling of track_buffer at the str level) used to graphically track a site's atomic movements in the OpenGL viewer during refinement/energy minimization. Set as a boolean equation, it controls when the site's current position is stored -- for example:

site Al1 ... track = Mod(Cycle_Iter, 2) == 0;

stores the Al1 site's position every second iteration. Applying this to every site in a structure (e.g. AlVO4) traces out each atom's trajectory as it moves during a simulated-annealing/energy-minimization run, useful for visually inspecting whether atoms are converging sensibly or wandering. See example ALVO4A.INP.

transform_X:
transform_X E, available on str, hkl_Is, xo_Is and d_Is phases, stretches an already-calculated phase pattern onto a transformed x-axis to form the final phase calculated pattern, without recalculating peaks or re-summing peaks into Ycalc -- for example:

prm tpx 0 transform_X = X + tpx Sin(X Pi / 360);

This is an approximation to using th2_offset instead, e.g.:

prm tpx 0 th2_offset = tpx Sin(X Pi/ 360);

and the approximation is accurate provided the change in transform_X is smooth and its largest value is of the order expected from typical zero-error/geometry corrections (such as those seen in XRD-CT data). The key advantage over th2_offset is object reuse: for two str's that share an identical calculated pattern but sit in different xdd's (e.g. because their instrument geometry or zero error differs), using th2_offset would require the peak summation into Ycalc to be performed twice, once per str, since th2_offset changes the pattern before it is calculated. transform_X instead applies the offset AFTER a single common str pattern has already been calculated and summed, so the same underlying calculated pattern can be reused and merely re-stretched onto each xdd's x-axis -- this is central to the object-reuse strategy described in section 6 (Reusing objects in large refinements), where phase_dets blocks such as:

phase_dets ze0 { transform_X = o10 + o20 X + X; }

are shared across large numbers of str's with only their transform_X differing, letting a single set of hkls/structure-factors/peak-buffer/Ycalc be computed once and reused with different per-str transform_X corrections applied afterwards -- refinements comprising 150,000 str's report this directly, e.g.:

Num transform_X/unique: 150000 75 In the GUI, hkl ticks displayed on Surface/1D/PlanView plots are automatically corrected for whichever of th2_offset or transform_X is in use. See example transform_X\tpx.inp.

user_y:
user_y $name { #include $file } (or user_y $name $file) loads an arbitrary, arbitrarily-spaced data file as a 'learnt shape' that can then be referenced by $name in equations that are a function of X, anywhere a fit_obj or peak convolution would otherwise be defined manually. user_y is used both for arbitrary background shapes and for peak shapes learnt directly from data -- example USER_Y\USER_Y.INP fits five fit_obj's to the quartz triplet using a single learnt peak shape defined via user_y, each instance independently scaled, positioned and stretched via fo_transform_X. Multiple user_y's can be defined, and each can be referenced any number of times, letting the same learnt shape be reused (with different scale/position) across a whole pattern; convergence is as fast as with any other refinement. user_y can also be used more generally to add, multiply or otherwise combine data files with different x-axis steps -- for example:

user_y f1 file1.xy; user_y f2 file2.xy; yobs_eqn result.sst = X (f1 + f2)^2;

adds two files, squares the result, and multiplies by X. Shapes can also be typed directly into the INP file using the _x1_dx tag rather than loaded from an external file -- a triangle, for example, is:

user_y NAME { _x1_dx -1 1  0 1 0 }

(start x-value and step, followed by the shape's y-values). xye_format and rebin_with_dx_of control how a loaded $file is interpreted; user_y_hat, user_y_gauss_fwhm, user_y_lor_fwhm and user_y_exp_conv_const are convolutions applied to the user_y data, identical in form to the ordinary peak convolutions hat, gauss_fwhm, lor_fwhm and exp_conv_const but scoped to a user_y rather than a phase peak (see example USER_Y\USER_Y_CONVOLUTION.INP).

xdd_file:
xdd_file sets the data-file name to load for an xdd that was created programmatically (via new $object -- see section 19.5, 'Creating many xdds at once using new and xdd_file') rather than declared with a literal xdd $file line. Although the manual's bracket notation for it is an equation ([xdd_file !E]), it is in practice assigned a STRING expression (typically built with Concat), not a plain number, since its job is to name a file. Used inside a for xdds { ... } loop right after the new(xdd, n) call that created the xdds, so each iteration can give its own xdd a different file name -- for example (section 2.13):

Create_XDDs(3)
prm i 1
for xdds {
    xdd_file = Concat("ceo2-", i, ".xdd")
    ...
    existing_prm i += 1;
}

where Create_XDDs(n) is: macro Create_XDDs(n) { move_to xdds move_to xdd_recs new(xdd, n) }. This loads three xdds -- ceo2-1.xdd, ceo2-2.xdd and ceo2-3.xdd -- each with its own structure and its own per-xdd parameters (e.g. beq values named via %Concat("bCe", i) etc.).

xdd_sum:
xdd_sum !E is similar to xdd_array, except that instead of storing a whole array of values, it calculates and stores the sum of those values -- both can be a function of the reserved parameter names X, Yobs, Ycalc and SigmaYobs. Like xdd_array, the summed value is only recalculated when its equation's dependencies change (and only at the point the xdd_sum is next referenced, not the instant the dependency changes), which keeps INP files using it fast and efficient. xdd_sum can be nested, which makes it useful for building normalized quantities across a whole pattern -- for example, to normalize the intensities between Yobs and Ycalc:

xdd_sum sum_yobs = Yobs;
xdd_sum sum_ycalc = Ycalc;
xdd_sum = (Yobs - Ycalc sum_yobs/sum_ycalc)^2;

It is also used to build a user-defined chi2 (see chi2), where xdd_sum expressions for a numerator and denominator are combined, e.g.:

xdd_sum numerator = (Yobs - Ycalc)^2 / Max(Yobs, 1);
xdd_sum denominator = Yobs;
chi2 = 100 Sqrt(numerator / denominator);

xdd_tag:
xdd_tag !E is an xdd-dependent keyword used with load_save_locals (see section 2.19, 'Automatically saving and loading parameters -- load_save_locals') to identify which saved parameter values belong to which xdd. Normally load_save_locals identifies parameters by the xdd file name (and, for RAW files, the range number) plus the phase name -- saving locally-named parameters (declared with $ inside a for {} loop) out to an INP_FILE.OUT_SL file and reloading them from INP_FILE.SL on a subsequent run. That identification breaks down when two xdds share the same file name (e.g. the same file loaded twice with different start_X/finish_X ranges); xdd_tag lets each xdd be tagged with its own distinguishing value instead, so its saved locals are correctly matched back up on reload. It is typically set via Load_Eval so the tag is evaluated once at load time, e.g.:

XDD(..\ceo2) finish_X 50 ...
XDD(..\ceo2) start_X  50 ...  ' same file name as the first xdd, needs xdd_tag
prm i 0
for xdds {
    xdd_tag = Load_Eval(i);
    existing_prm i += 1;
    ...
}

Phase names within a given xdd must still be unique. See also test_examples\load-save-locals\lsl.inp.
